---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { breadcrumbs, website } from '../seo/jsonld';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Methodology and sources · Scintille"
	description="Where Scintille's lightning comes from, what the satellites measure, how fast it arrives, how brightness is set, and what the globe cannot show."
	type="article"
	jsonLd={[
		website(),
		breadcrumbs([
			{ name: 'Scintille', path: '/' },
			{ name: 'Methodology and sources', path: '/methodology/' },
		]),
	]}
>
	<main>
		<p><a href="/">← Scintille</a></p>
		<h1>Methodology and sources</h1>
		<p class="lead">
			What the globe shows, where it comes from, and where it falls short. Everything here describes what the
			pipeline does today; parts that are still being built say so.
		</p>

		<nav aria-label="On this page">
			<a href="#measured">What is measured</a>
			<a href="#sources">Sources</a>
			<a href="#coverage">Coverage</a>
			<a href="#latency">Latency</a>
			<a href="#brightness">Brightness</a>
			<a href="#overlap">Overlap</a>
			<a href="#quality">Quality</a>
			<a href="#rendering">On screen</a>
			<a href="#data">Open data</a>
			<a href="#limits">Limits</a>
			<a href="#attribution">Attribution</a>
		</nav>

		<h2 id="measured">What is measured</h2>
		<p>
			The instruments behind Scintille are lightning imagers on geostationary satellites. They stare at the same
			disc of the Earth day and night and record the brief optical pulses that lightning makes at the top of the
			clouds. They see flashes inside a cloud and between clouds as well as those that reach the ground, and the
			products used here do not tell them apart. Scintille is therefore not a map of ground strikes.
		</p>
		<p>
			One dot on the globe is one <strong>flash</strong> as defined by the source product: built by the source's own
			processing from the optical events it detected, from the first to the last. For each flash Scintille keeps:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>the time the satellite saw it start, to the microsecond;</li>
			<li>the latitude and longitude of its centroid;</li>
			<li>its optical energy, exactly as the source reports it, with its unit;</li>
			<li>the area it covered and its duration;</li>
			<li>quality caveats, such as a flash the source itself marks as degraded.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Every flash gets a stable identifier derived from its source, its product file and its id in that file, so
			reprocessing the same file never creates a second copy.
		</p>

		<h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr><th scope="col">Instrument</th><th scope="col">Position</th><th scope="col">Product</th><th scope="col">Access</th></tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr>
					<td>GLM on GOES-East</td>
					<td>75.2° W</td>
					<td>NOAA GLM L2 LCFA, one file every 20 s</td>
					<td>NOAA open data bucket on AWS</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>GLM on GOES-West</td>
					<td>137.0° W</td>
					<td>NOAA GLM L2 LCFA, one file every 20 s</td>
					<td>NOAA open data bucket on AWS</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>Lightning Imager on MTG-I1</td>
					<td>0°</td>
					<td>EUMETSAT LI L2 LFL, one file every 30 s</td>
					<td>EUMETSAT Data Store, collection EO:EUM:DAT:0691</td>
				</tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
		<p>
			The <a href="https://www.goes-r.gov/spacesegment/glm.html">Geostationary Lightning Mapper</a> (GLM) flies on
			NOAA's GOES-East and GOES-West satellites and covers the Americas and the surrounding oceans.
			The <a href="https://www.eumetsat.int/meteosat-third-generation">MTG Lightning Imager</a> (LI) flies on
			EUMETSAT's Meteosat Third Generation and covers Europe, Africa and most of the Atlantic Ocean.
		</p>
		<p class="pending">
			The MTG Lightning Imager feed is still being connected. This section will describe its ingestion once it
			runs in production.
		</p>

		<h2 id="coverage">Coverage</h2>
		<p>
			Each instrument sees one side of the planet from its fixed point above the equator. Together, GOES-East,
			GOES-West and MTG span the Americas, the eastern Pacific, the Atlantic, Europe and Africa. Asia, Oceania and
			the polar regions are seen by none of them.
			An empty region on the globe can mean that no lightning occurred, or that no satellite here was looking: read
			it with this map in mind.
		</p>
		<p>
			Detection is not uniform inside a field of view either. Towards the edge of the disc, the satellite sees the
			clouds at a grazing angle and its detection efficiency drops, so weak flashes are missed more often there.
			Over the last 30 days measured, GLM recorded flashes up to 67° of arc from the point below each satellite.
		</p>
		<p class="pending">
			A coverage map, drawn from the published footprint of each source, will be added here.
		</p>

		<h2 id="latency">Latency</h2>
		<p>
			A flash reaches the globe in three steps. The satellite operator turns raw observations into a product file
			and publishes it: on GLM this was measured at about 30 s. Scintille's ingestion service picks the file up,
			parses it and adds its flashes to the live feed, which is published as one chunk every 30 s. The browser then
			reads the newest chunks. The target is that 95 % of flashes are published within 120 s of the time the
			satellite saw them.
		</p>
		<p>
			Latency is measured continuously, per source, from the flash time to its publication, and shown on
			the <a href="/status/">status page</a> with the median and 95th percentile over the last hour.
		</p>
		<p>
			A source is shown as <em>live</em> while its files keep arriving, <em>delayed</em> when none has arrived for
			2 minutes, and <em>offline</em> after 10. If the feed as a whole is more than 3 minutes old, the globe stops
			calling itself live.
		</p>
		<p class="pending">
			The latency of the MTG Lightning Imager will be given here once it has been measured in production.
		</p>

		<h2 id="brightness">Brightness</h2>
		<p>
			The brightness of a dot follows the optical energy the satellite measured for that flash. The two instruments
			do not report the same quantity: GLM gives an energy in joules, the Lightning Imager a radiance in
			mW m⁻² sr⁻¹. Scintille stores both values as reported and derives from them a
			common <em>intensity</em> between 0 and 1, which is what the globe draws.
		</p>
		<p>
			Today that intensity is a provisional mapping on a logarithmic scale: for GLM, 10⁻¹⁵·⁵ J maps to 0 and
			10⁻¹¹·⁵ J to 1; for the Lightning Imager, 1 to 10,000 mW m⁻² sr⁻¹. On screen, the faintest flash is drawn at
			15 % of the brightness of the strongest so that it stays visible.
		</p>
		<p class="pending">
			A calibration between the two instruments, based on their measured distributions, will replace this mapping.
			Its method and limits will be described here.
		</p>

		<h2 id="overlap">Overlap between satellites</h2>
		<p>
			GOES-East and GOES-West both see the Americas between about 140° W and 70° W, so most flashes there are
			recorded twice, a few kilometres apart. To draw each flash once, every point on the globe is assigned to the
			instrument with the best viewing angle, the one whose point below the satellite is closest. Between the two
			GOES satellites the boundary is the meridian at 106.1° W.
		</p>
		<p>
			A flash seen by an instrument outside its own zone is kept in the archive with a flag and left out of the
			live globe. Over 30 days of GLM data (September to October 2026, 64.2 million flashes), 20.9 % were flagged
			this way. If the owner of a zone delivers no file for 5 minutes, the other satellite's flashes there come
			back on the globe, so an outage does not leave the zone dark.
		</p>
		<p class="pending">
			The overlap between GOES-East and the MTG Lightning Imager over the Atlantic will be handled the same way;
			this section will be completed when it is.
		</p>

		<h2 id="quality">Quality checks</h2>
		<p>
			Every file is checked against the data contract when it is read: positions, times, energies and flags must
			be in range. Rows that fail are not silently dropped; they are set aside with the reason, and a file that
			cannot be read is logged as failed. Each hour, the archive is checked for freshness, for completeness
			(at least 99 % of the expected product files), for value ranges and for unusual volumes; each day, for
			duplicates and for an exact match between files received, flashes parsed and flashes stored.
		</p>
		<p>
			Some GLM detector pixels fire at a fixed position with no storm around, sometimes tens of thousands of times a
			day. Since late September 2026 the strongest one sits off West Africa, near 21.3° N 28.7° W.
			These <em>hot spots</em> are kept in the data, faithful to the source, and can appear on the globe as a dot that
			keeps flashing in the same place. Storm detection leaves them out: flashes within 10 km of a detected hot spot do not count
			towards storms, so storm statistics exclude them.
		</p>

		<h2 id="rendering">On screen</h2>
		<p>
			The globe animates flashes by the time the satellite saw them, not the time they arrived. Each flash
			flickers for its measured duration, held between 60 and 600 ms, then leaves an afterglow whose length you
			choose (1 to 60 minutes). The size of its halo follows the area it covered. The day and night sides follow
			the position of the Sun at the time shown.
		</p>
		<p>
			The cloud layer comes from NOAA's hourly global infrared mosaic. It lags the lightning by 40 to 100 minutes,
			so a storm that grows fast can flash before its cloud appears.
		</p>
		<p>
			The optional thunder is synthesized in the browser. Its delay uses a stylized speed of sound of 6000 km/s,
			capped at 4 s: at the real 0.34 km/s, thunder seen from orbit would arrive hours later. The delay only says
			whether a flash is near or far.
		</p>

		<h2 id="data">Open data</h2>
		<p>
			The live feed is public: <a href="https://data.scintille.app/live/manifest.json">a manifest</a> lists the
			chunks of the last hour, each holding the flashes received during 30 s with their time, position, intensity,
			area, duration, source and quality flags. Its format is versioned and documented in the project's data
			contract.
		</p>

		<h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>Large parts of the planet have no coverage; an empty region is not proof that there was no lightning.</li>
			<li>Optical imagers miss some flashes, more so near the edge of their view, and do not separate cloud flashes from ground strikes.</li>
			<li>Brightness is a relative scale, not a physical unit, and the scale is still provisional.</li>
			<li>A few fixed GLM pixels produce false flashes that stay on the globe.</li>
			<li>Near the boundary between two satellites, a flash can occasionally be drawn twice or not at all, in a band a few kilometres wide.</li>
			<li>The live feed covers the last hour; older flashes are kept in the archive only.</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="attribution">Attribution</h2>
		<ul class="credits">
			<li>GLM data courtesy of NOAA.</li>
			<li>Contains modified EUMETSAT Meteosat data 2026.</li>
			<li>Clouds: NOAA NESDIS GMGSI (contains EUMETSAT Meteosat and JMA Himawari data).</li>
			<li>Earth imagery: NASA Earth Observatory (Blue Marble and Black Marble).</li>
			<li>Land and water mask: Natural Earth.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			GLM data is public domain. EUMETSAT Lightning Imager data is distributed under the EUMETSAT Data Policy
			(<a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>). Anyone reusing the live feed must keep
			these credits.
		</p>
	</main>
</BaseLayout>

